{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JAwWBU88trBwNJMbbwQU51","type":"tool_result","content":"0\t## Iteration US-162 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n1\t\n2\tCompleted: Add React 19.2 cacheSignal() for Server Component fetch cleanup (ID: US-162)\n3\t\n4\t### What was implemented\n5\t- Created `lib/cache-utils.ts` with two functions:\n6\t  - `getCachedData()`: Generic utility that wraps fetch with cache() and cacheSignal()\n7\t  - `getCachedRoomAvailability()`: Example implementation for room availability API calls\n8\t- Updated `Rooms.tsx` Server Component to import and demonstrate cacheSignal() pattern\n9\t- Added try-catch error handling to gracefully handle AbortError when cache is invalidated\n10\t- No TypeScript errors; all 83 pages generated successfully during build\n11\t\n12\t### Patterns discovered\n13\t- React 19.2 cacheSignal() is only available in Server Components (not Client Components or Route Handlers)\n14\t- cacheSignal() returns an AbortSignal that fires when cache lifetime ends\n15\t- Pattern: `const dedupedFetch = cache((url) => fetch(url, { signal: cacheSignal() }))`\n16\t- AbortError has name property that can be checked to distinguish cache invalidation from network errors\n17\t- Graceful error handling prevents cache-related aborts from breaking the page\n18\t\n19\t### Gotchas\n20\t- cacheSignal() is a React 19.2 feature; requires Next.js 16+ for full support\n21\t- Must import both `cache` and `cacheSignal` from \"react\" (not from \"next/cache\")\n22\t- External API calls may fail during SSR (expected for example APIs) — always wrap in try-catch\n23\t\n24\t## Iteration US-161 (Attempt 3) - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n25\t\n26\tCompleted: Implement React 19.2 useEffectEvent for stable analytics event handlers (ID: US-161)\n27\t\n28\t### What was implemented\n29\t- Replaced `useCallback` with `useEffectEvent` in RoomsEcommerceTracker.tsx and BookEcommerceTracker.tsx\n30\t- Removed analytics click handlers from useEffect dependency arrays\n31\t- Effects now run once on mount, not on every callback identity change\n32\t\n33\t### Patterns discovered\n34\t- Code was already committed in attempt 2 (d9fa754) but diff guard failed due to unstaged files inflating diff count\n35\t- Attempt 3 only needed to verify code was in place and mark passes: true\n36\t- When prior attempts commit code but fail post-commit gates, verify existing commits before re-implementing\n37\t\n38\t### Gotchas\n39\t- SPIRAL diff guard counts ALL staged/unstaged changes, not just story-related files — always use `git add <specific-files>` not `git add -A`\n40\t- Previous attempts failed at 540 and 23068 lines because unrelated modified files were included in the diff measurement\n41\t\n42\t## Iteration US-144 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n43\t\n44\tCompleted: Replace JavaScript tooltip positioning with CSS Popover API and Anchor Positioning (ID: US-144)\n45\t\n46\t### What was implemented\n47\t- Created `InfoPopover.tsx` component using native HTML `popover` attribute, `popoverTarget`, CSS `anchor-name`/`position-anchor`\n48\t- Added popover + anchor positioning CSS in globals.css with `@supports` fallback for browsers without anchor positioning\n49\t- Converted Header.tsx search button `title` tooltip to InfoPopover\n50\t- Converted HeroTrustBadges.tsx trust badge buttons to InfoPopover with contextual content\n51\t- No JS tooltip library existed to remove (no Popper.js/Floating UI in the project)\n52\t\n53\t### Patterns discovered\n54\t- React 19 includes `popover` and `popoverTarget` in its type definitions — no `@ts-expect-error` needed\n55\t- `CSS.escape()` is a browser API not available in Node.js SSR — use regex sanitization instead for `useId()` output\n56\t- Attempt 1 failed with 17,788 line diff — caused by touching package-lock.json or similar large files\n57\t- Keep implementation under 500 lines total insertions+deletions\n58\t\n59\t### Gotchas\n60\t- `useId()` returns colon-wrapped strings like `:r0:` — must sanitize for CSS custom property names\n61\t- Linter may auto-revert changes if import is added separately from usage\n62\t\n63\t## Iteration US-143 (FINAL - Attempt 3) - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n64\t\n65\tCompleted: Apply CSS @starting-style for GPU-accelerated entry animations (ID: US-143)\n66\t\n67\t### What was implemented\n68\t- Added @starting-style CSS utility classes (.entry-fade, .entry-zoom-in, .entry-fade-up) to globals.css\n69\t- Classes use only opacity + transform for GPU compositor thread\n70\t- Includes prefers-reduced-motion: reduce override that disables transitions\n71\t- Components modified in commit b3c2354: CookieConsent, ExitIntentOverlay, RoomsTabsClient, SearchDialog\n72\t- CSS classes added in commit 9d11b13\n73\t\n74\t### Patterns discovered\n75\t- Attempt 1 failed: oversized diff (14829 lines > 500 limit)\n76\t- Attempt 2 failed: missing diagnosis block (process gate)\n77\t- Attempt 3 succeeded: code was already committed, just needed verification and passes=true\n78\t- When implementation is already committed, verify in-place rather than re-implementing\n79\t\n80\t### Gotchas\n81\t- SPIRAL may revert prd.json passes flag between attempts even if code is committed\n82\t- The TurnstileWidget TS error is pre-existing (missing @marsidev/react-turnstile dep) and unrelated\n83\t- Always output diagnosis block BEFORE any file edits to pass SPIRAL process gate\n84\t\n85\t## Iteration US-249 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n86\t\n87\tCompleted: Blog post - 5 Gig Jobs for Malaysians Waiting for Singapore Work Approval (ID: US-249)\n88\t\n89\t### What was implemented\n90\t- **Blog post** (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`):\n91\t  - Added new blog post object with slug `gig-jobs-malaysia-waiting-singapore-work-pass`\n92\t  - Category: \"Career & Work\", date: \"2026-04-14\", readTime: \"9 min read\"\n93\t  - 5 gig economy job options with earnings estimates:\n94\t    - Foodpanda Delivery Rider: RM1,800–3,500/month\n95\t    - Lalamove Driver: RM2,000–3,800/month\n96\t    - Grab Ride/GrabFood Driver: RM2,500–4,200/month\n97\t    - Part-time F&B/Cafe Work: RM1,500–2,500/month\n98\t    - Online Freelancing: RM1,200–5,000+/month\n99\t  - Each job includes: requirements, earnings breakdown, pros, and cons\n100\t  - Sections on combining gigs, tax/legal notes, Pelangi guest relevance\n101\t  - Internal links to `/rooms` (Pelangi Capsule)\n102\t  - Intro references Pelangi guests in work pass waiting period\n103\t\n104\t### Patterns discovered\n105\t- Blog post HTML content needs careful optimization to stay under 8000 character limit\n106\t- Initial draft (8191 chars) required condensing descriptions by ~35% to fit limit\n107\t- Final optimized content: 5315 characters (well within limit)\n108\t- Consistent format: metadata + HTML content with proper spacing and paragraph structure\n109\t\n110\t### Test results\n111\t- Content size: 5315 characters (under 8000 limit) ✓\n112\t- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n113\t- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (all static pages generated)\n114\t- All internal links present: `/rooms` (2 times) ✓\n115\t- All acceptance criteria met:\n116\t  - ✓ Slug 'gig-jobs-malaysia-waiting-singapore-work-pass', category 'Career & Work'\n117\t  - ✓ 5 gig job options with earnings estimates\n118\t  - ✓ Intro references Pelangi guests\n119\t  - ✓ readTime '9 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n120\t  - ✓ npm run build passes\n121\t\n122\t## Iteration US-244 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n123\t\n124\tCompleted: Blog post - Living in JB Working in Singapore — Cross-Border Commuter Guide 2026 (ID: US-244)\n125\t\n126\t### What was implemented\n127\t- **Blog post** (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`):\n128\t  - Added new blog post object with slug `jb-singapore-commuter-guide-2026`\n129\t  - Category: \"Career & Work\", date: \"2026-04-14\", readTime: \"8 min read\"\n130\t  - Comprehensive HTML table of 5 bus services with routes, prices, duration, and frequency:\n131\t    - Causeway Link: RM5–6 / SGD2–2.50\n132\t    - SBS Transit: RM3–4 / SGD1.50\n133\t    - Sunrays Express: RM5 / SGD2\n134\t    - Premium (Intercity): RM8–12 / SGD4–5\n135\t    - Personal vehicle: RM30–50 (toll + petrol)\n136\t  - Monthly cost breakdown table with 7 expense categories (rent, transport, food, utilities, phone, sundries)\n137\t  - Shows monthly savings of SGD4,265–4,665 (SGD50,000–56,000 annually)\n138\t  - Sections on CIQ process, peak hour tips, housing strategy, commute sustainability\n139\t  - Internal links to `/rooms` (Pelangi Capsule) and `/getting-here` for directions\n140\t\n141\t### Patterns discovered\n142\t- Blog post HTML content remains under 8000 character limit (7,378 chars in this case)\n143\t- Reading time is auto-computed from HTML word count via `computeReadingTime()` function\n144\t- Blog slugs use kebab-case format with date suffix for blog posts\n145\t- Tables provide structured comparison and cost data for complex topics\n146\t\n147\t### Test results\n148\t- Content size: 7,378 characters (well under 8,000 limit)\n149\t- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n150\t- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (68 static pages including new blog post)\n151\t- All internal links present: `/rooms` (3 times), `/getting-here` (1 time)\n152\t- All acceptance criteria met:\n153\t  - ✓ Slug 'jb-singapore-commuter-guide-2026', category 'Career & Work'\n154\t  - ✓ HTML list of 5 bus services with prices and details\n155\t  - ✓ Monthly cost breakdown with 7 expense categories\n156\t  - ✓ Internal links to /getting-here and /rooms\n157\t  - ✓ readTime '8 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n158\t  - ✓ npm run build passes\n159\t\n160\t## Iteration US-243 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n161\t\n162\tCompleted: Blog post - Singapore Work Pass Guide for Malaysians (ID: US-243)\n163\t\n164\t### What was implemented\n165\t- **Blog post** (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`):\n166\t  - Added new blog post object with slug `singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians`\n167\t  - Category: \"Career & Work\", date: \"2026-04-14\", readTime: \"12 min read\"\n168\t  - Comprehensive HTML comparison table of 3 Singapore work pass types:\n169\t    - Employment Pass (EP): Minimum salary SGD 5,000/month\n170\t    - S Pass: Minimum salary SGD 3,150/month\n171\t    - Work Permit: No fixed minimum, sector-specific\n172\t  - 5 Q&A items in FAQ section covering common questions:\n173\t    - Diploma vs EP eligibility\n174\t    - Salary negotiation strategy\n175\t    - Family dependencies policies\n176\t    - Actual processing timelines\n177\t    - Job changing mid-pass restrictions\n178\t  - Additional sections on red flags, individual pass details, and next steps\n179\t\n180\t### Patterns discovered\n181\t- Blog posts are stored as static TypeScript objects in `rawPosts` array\n182\t- Reading time is auto-computed from word count (200 wpm baseline)\n183\t- HTML content is embedded as template literals in TypeScript strings\n184\t- Blog metadata includes slug, title, excerpt, date, category, author, readTime, tags, featuredImage\n185\t\n186\t### Gotchas\n187\t- Content HTML must stay under 8000 characters for performance\n188\t- All blog post fields are required; missing fields cause TypeScript compilation errors\n189\t- Category must match existing categories or create new ones (checked against unique set)\n190\t\n191\t### Test results\n192\t- Content size: 6,066 characters (well under 8,000 limit)\n193\t- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n194\t- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (67 static pages including new blog post)\n195\t- Blog post accessible via /blog/singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians slug\n196\t- All acceptance criteria met:\n197\t  - ✓ Slug and category correct\n198\t  - ✓ Comparison table with all 3 pass types and salary minimums\n199\t  - ✓ FAQ section with 5 Q&As (exceeds 3 required)\n200\t  - ✓ ReadTime '12 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n201\t  - ✓ npm run build passes\n202\t\n203\t## Iteration US-196 - Fri, Apr 14, 2026\n204\t\n205\tCompleted: Integrate channel manager for live OTA availability synchronisation (ID: US-196)\n206\t\n207\t### What was implemented\n208\t- **Beds24 client** (`site/src/lib/beds24-client.ts`):\n209\t  - Implemented `parseAvailabilityResponse()` to parse real Beds24 API responses\n210\t  - Returns proper `ChannelManagerAvailability` objects with room inventory\n211\t  - Graceful fallback to placeholder data when channel manager is not configured\n212\t- **ARI endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/ari/route.ts`):\n213\t  - Fetches live availability from Beds24 API\n214\t  - Maps to standard ARI format for connectivity partners (Google Free Booking Links)\n215\t  - Returns JSON with property info, rooms, rates, and booking URL\n216\t- **Booking sync endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/bookings/sync/route.ts`):\n217\t  - Accepts POST requests with booking details (checkin, checkout, guest info, room type)\n218\t  - Syncs bookings to Beds24 to close availability across OTAs\n219\t  - Includes availability check via GET with query parameters\n220\t- **Webhook endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/webhooks/beds24/route.ts`):\n221\t  - Processes Beds24 webhook events for availability updates, booking creation/cancellation\n222\t  - Validates webhook signature using HMAC-SHA256\n223\t  - Logs event details (extensible for database updates)\n224\t- **Configuration & types**:\n225\t  - Channel manager config with environment variables\n226\t  - Type definitions for availability, bookings, webhooks\n227\t  - Updated .env.local.example with all required secrets\n228\t\n229\t### Patterns discovered\n230\t- Next.js App Router uses async GET/POST functions\n231\t- Beds24 API response structure uses `roomtypes` key with room availability objects\n232\t- Response parsing should handle multiple field names (id/roomId, name/roomName) for flexibility\n233\t- Placeholder data allows testing without configured channel manager\n234\t\n235\t### Gotchas\n236\t- Beds24 API field names vary (id vs roomId, total vs totalInventory)\n237\t- Parser must be defensive and return empty array on malformed responses\n238\t- Webhook validation using HMAC requires exact payload matching (must use `request.text()` not `request.json()`)\n239\t\n240\t### Test results\n241\t- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n242\t- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (all 65 static pages generated)\n243\t- ARI endpoint: ✓ shows as dynamic route (ƒ)\n244\t- Webhook endpoint: ✓ shows as dynamic route (ƒ)\n245\t- Booking sync endpoint: ✓ shows as dynamic route (ƒ)\n246\t\n247\t## Pre-analysis for US-196 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\n248\tThis user story is for a hotel channel manager integration, which seems mismatched with the `lhdn_payroll_integration` app context. The following brief assumes the target is a hospitality-focused Frappe app (e.g., `pelangi_hospitality`).\n249\t\n250\t**Project:** US-196: Channel Manager Integration\n251\t**Target App:** `pelangi_hospitality` (Assumed)\n252\t\n253\t**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n254\t*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/channel_manager.py` to encapsulate all communication with the third-party channel manager API (e.g., Beds24).\n255\t*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/webhooks.py` to create a dedicated, whitelisted endpoint to receive incoming ARI updates and bookings from the channel manager.\n256\t*   **Existing File:** `pelangi_hospitality/doctype/booking/booking.py` (or similar for reservations) to add logic that pushes new bookings to the channel manager.\n257\t\n258\t**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n259\t*   **DocTypes:**\n260\t    *   A new singleton `Channel Manager Settings` DocType is needed to store API keys, endpoint URLs, and credentials.\n261\t    *   The `Booking` DocType will require modification to trigger syncs and store the channel manager's external booking ID.\n262\t    *   The `Room` DocType needs a field to map its ID to the corresponding ID in the channel manager.\n263\t*   **Hooks (`hooks.py`):**\n264\t    *   `on_submit` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and reduce availability.\n265\t    *   `on_cancel` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and increase availability.\n266\t\n267\t**3. Implementation Approach:**\n268\t1.  Implement the API client with methods for fetching and updating availability.\n269\t2.  Use the `Booking` DocType's server-side `after_insert` and `on_trash` events to trigger API calls.\n270\t3.  Create a whitelisted API endpoint for the website frontend to call, which in turn fetches live availability from the channel manager API.\n271\t4.  Implement the webhook endpoint to create/update local `Booking` documents from incoming OTA bookings.\n272\t\n273\t**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n274\t*   **Race Conditions:** Implement a locking mechanism or re-verify availability with the channel manager API just before creating a booking to prevent overbookings.\n275\t*   **API Failure:** Use Frappe's background jobs (RQ) with a retry mechanism for all outgoing API calls to handle transient network issues or API downtime.\n276\t*   **Webhook Security:** Validate incoming webhooks using a shared secret to prevent unauthorized requests.\n277\t\n278\t\n279\t## Pre-analysis for US-196 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\n280\tThis user story is for a hotel channel manager integration, which seems mismatched with the `lhdn_payroll_integration` app context. The following brief assumes the target is a hospitality-focused Frappe app (e.g., `pelangi_hospitality`).\n281\t\n282\t**Project:** US-196: Channel Manager Integration\n283\t**Target App:** `pelangi_hospitality` (Assumed)\n284\t\n285\t**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n286\t*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/channel_manager.py` to encapsulate all communication with the third-party channel manager API (e.g., Beds24).\n287\t*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/webhooks.py` to create a dedicated, whitelisted endpoint to receive incoming ARI updates and bookings from the channel manager.\n288\t*   **Existing File:** `pelangi_hospitality/doctype/booking/booking.py` (or similar for reservations) to add logic that pushes new bookings to the channel manager.\n289\t\n290\t**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n291\t*   **DocTypes:**\n292\t    *   A new singleton `Channel Manager Settings` DocType is needed to store API keys, endpoint URLs, and credentials.\n293\t    *   The `Booking` DocType will require modification to trigger syncs and store the channel manager's external booking ID.\n294\t    *   The `Room` DocType needs a field to map its ID to the corresponding ID in the channel manager.\n295\t*   **Hooks (`hooks.py`):**\n296\t    *   `on_submit` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and reduce availability.\n297\t    *   `on_cancel` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and increase availability.\n298\t\n299\t**3. Implementation Approach:**\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5db64454-1df5-4c1c-9e81-ebba85b5ca40","uuid":"274bd5ea-3f78-4090-b110-a5ef930b8c4c","timestamp":"2026-04-14T06:18:54.382Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\progress.txt","content":"## Iteration US-162 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Add React 19.2 cacheSignal() for Server Component fetch cleanup (ID: US-162)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `lib/cache-utils.ts` with two functions:\n  - `getCachedData()`: Generic utility that wraps fetch with cache() and cacheSignal()\n  - `getCachedRoomAvailability()`: Example implementation for room availability API calls\n- Updated `Rooms.tsx` Server Component to import and demonstrate cacheSignal() pattern\n- Added try-catch error handling to gracefully handle AbortError when cache is invalidated\n- No TypeScript errors; all 83 pages generated successfully during build\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- React 19.2 cacheSignal() is only available in Server Components (not Client Components or Route Handlers)\n- cacheSignal() returns an AbortSignal that fires when cache lifetime ends\n- Pattern: `const dedupedFetch = cache((url) => fetch(url, { signal: cacheSignal() }))`\n- AbortError has name property that can be checked to distinguish cache invalidation from network errors\n- Graceful error handling prevents cache-related aborts from breaking the page\n\n### Gotchas\n- cacheSignal() is a React 19.2 feature; requires Next.js 16+ for full support\n- Must import both `cache` and `cacheSignal` from \"react\" (not from \"next/cache\")\n- External API calls may fail during SSR (expected for example APIs) — always wrap in try-catch\n\n## Iteration US-161 (Attempt 3) - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Implement React 19.2 useEffectEvent for stable analytics event handlers (ID: US-161)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Replaced `useCallback` with `useEffectEvent` in RoomsEcommerceTracker.tsx and BookEcommerceTracker.tsx\n- Removed analytics click handlers from useEffect dependency arrays\n- Effects now run once on mount, not on every callback identity change\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Code was already committed in attempt 2 (d9fa754) but diff guard failed due to unstaged files inflating diff count\n- Attempt 3 only needed to verify code was in place and mark passes: true\n- When prior attempts commit code but fail post-commit gates, verify existing commits before re-implementing\n\n### Gotchas\n- SPIRAL diff guard counts ALL staged/unstaged changes, not just story-related files — always use `git add <specific-files>` not `git add -A`\n- Previous attempts failed at 540 and 23068 lines because unrelated modified files were included in the diff measurement\n\n## Iteration US-144 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Replace JavaScript tooltip positioning with CSS Popover API and Anchor Positioning (ID: US-144)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `InfoPopover.tsx` component using native HTML `popover` attribute, `popoverTarget`, CSS `anchor-name`/`position-anchor`\n- Added popover + anchor positioning CSS in globals.css with `@supports` fallback for browsers without anchor positioning\n- Converted Header.tsx search button `title` tooltip to InfoPopover\n- Converted HeroTrustBadges.tsx trust badge buttons to InfoPopover with contextual content\n- No JS tooltip library existed to remove (no Popper.js/Floating UI in the project)\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- React 19 includes `popover` and `popoverTarget` in its type definitions — no `@ts-expect-error` needed\n- `CSS.escape()` is a browser API not available in Node.js SSR — use regex sanitization instead for `useId()` output\n- Attempt 1 failed with 17,788 line diff — caused by touching package-lock.json or similar large files\n- Keep implementation under 500 lines total insertions+deletions\n\n### Gotchas\n- `useId()` returns colon-wrapped strings like `:r0:` — must sanitize for CSS custom property names\n- Linter may auto-revert changes if import is added separately from usage\n\n## Iteration US-143 (FINAL - Attempt 3) - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Apply CSS @starting-style for GPU-accelerated entry animations (ID: US-143)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Added @starting-style CSS utility classes (.entry-fade, .entry-zoom-in, .entry-fade-up) to globals.css\n- Classes use only opacity + transform for GPU compositor thread\n- Includes prefers-reduced-motion: reduce override that disables transitions\n- Components modified in commit b3c2354: CookieConsent, ExitIntentOverlay, RoomsTabsClient, SearchDialog\n- CSS classes added in commit 9d11b13\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Attempt 1 failed: oversized diff (14829 lines > 500 limit)\n- Attempt 2 failed: missing diagnosis block (process gate)\n- Attempt 3 succeeded: code was already committed, just needed verification and passes=true\n- When implementation is already committed, verify in-place rather than re-implementing\n\n### Gotchas\n- SPIRAL may revert prd.json passes flag between attempts even if code is committed\n- The TurnstileWidget TS error is pre-existing (missing @marsidev/react-turnstile dep) and unrelated\n- Always output diagnosis block BEFORE any file edits to pass SPIRAL process gate\n\n## Iteration US-249 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Blog post - 5 Gig Jobs for Malaysians Waiting for Singapore Work Approval (ID: US-249)\n\n### What was implemented\n- **Blog post** (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`):\n  - Added new blog post object with slug `gig-jobs-malaysia-waiting-singapore-work-pass`\n  - Category: \"Career & Work\", date: \"2026-04-14\", readTime: \"9 min read\"\n  - 5 gig economy job options with earnings estimates:\n    - Foodpanda Delivery Rider: RM1,800–3,500/month\n    - Lalamove Driver: RM2,000–3,800/month\n    - Grab Ride/GrabFood Driver: RM2,500–4,200/month\n    - Part-time F&B/Cafe Work: RM1,500–2,500/month\n    - Online Freelancing: RM1,200–5,000+/month\n  - Each job includes: requirements, earnings breakdown, pros, and cons\n  - Sections on combining gigs, tax/legal notes, Pelangi guest relevance\n  - Internal links to `/rooms` (Pelangi Capsule)\n  - Intro references Pelangi guests in work pass waiting period\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Blog post HTML content needs careful optimization to stay under 8000 character limit\n- Initial draft (8191 chars) required condensing descriptions by ~35% to fit limit\n- Final optimized content: 5315 characters (well within limit)\n- Consistent format: metadata + HTML content with proper spacing and paragraph structure\n\n### Test results\n- Content size: 5315 characters (under 8000 limit) ✓\n- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (all static pages generated)\n- All internal links present: `/rooms` (2 times) ✓\n- All acceptance criteria met:\n  - ✓ Slug 'gig-jobs-malaysia-waiting-singapore-work-pass', category 'Career & Work'\n  - ✓ 5 gig job options with earnings estimates\n  - ✓ Intro references Pelangi guests\n  - ✓ readTime '9 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n  - ✓ npm run build passes\n\n## Iteration US-244 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Blog post - Living in JB Working in Singapore — Cross-Border Commuter Guide 2026 (ID: US-244)\n\n### What was implemented\n- **Blog post** (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`):\n  - Added new blog post object with slug `jb-singapore-commuter-guide-2026`\n  - Category: \"Career & Work\", date: \"2026-04-14\", readTime: \"8 min read\"\n  - Comprehensive HTML table of 5 bus services with routes, prices, duration, and frequency:\n    - Causeway Link: RM5–6 / SGD2–2.50\n    - SBS Transit: RM3–4 / SGD1.50\n    - Sunrays Express: RM5 / SGD2\n    - Premium (Intercity): RM8–12 / SGD4–5\n    - Personal vehicle: RM30–50 (toll + petrol)\n  - Monthly cost breakdown table with 7 expense categories (rent, transport, food, utilities, phone, sundries)\n  - Shows monthly savings of SGD4,265–4,665 (SGD50,000–56,000 annually)\n  - Sections on CIQ process, peak hour tips, housing strategy, commute sustainability\n  - Internal links to `/rooms` (Pelangi Capsule) and `/getting-here` for directions\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Blog post HTML content remains under 8000 character limit (7,378 chars in this case)\n- Reading time is auto-computed from HTML word count via `computeReadingTime()` function\n- Blog slugs use kebab-case format with date suffix for blog posts\n- Tables provide structured comparison and cost data for complex topics\n\n### Test results\n- Content size: 7,378 characters (well under 8,000 limit)\n- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (68 static pages including new blog post)\n- All internal links present: `/rooms` (3 times), `/getting-here` (1 time)\n- All acceptance criteria met:\n  - ✓ Slug 'jb-singapore-commuter-guide-2026', category 'Career & Work'\n  - ✓ HTML list of 5 bus services with prices and details\n  - ✓ Monthly cost breakdown with 7 expense categories\n  - ✓ Internal links to /getting-here and /rooms\n  - ✓ readTime '8 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n  - ✓ npm run build passes\n\n## Iteration US-243 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Blog post - Singapore Work Pass Guide for Malaysians (ID: US-243)\n\n### What was implemented\n- **Blog post** (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`):\n  - Added new blog post object with slug `singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians`\n  - Category: \"Career & Work\", date: \"2026-04-14\", readTime: \"12 min read\"\n  - Comprehensive HTML comparison table of 3 Singapore work pass types:\n    - Employment Pass (EP): Minimum salary SGD 5,000/month\n    - S Pass: Minimum salary SGD 3,150/month\n    - Work Permit: No fixed minimum, sector-specific\n  - 5 Q&A items in FAQ section covering common questions:\n    - Diploma vs EP eligibility\n    - Salary negotiation strategy\n    - Family dependencies policies\n    - Actual processing timelines\n    - Job changing mid-pass restrictions\n  - Additional sections on red flags, individual pass details, and next steps\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Blog posts are stored as static TypeScript objects in `rawPosts` array\n- Reading time is auto-computed from word count (200 wpm baseline)\n- HTML content is embedded as template literals in TypeScript strings\n- Blog metadata includes slug, title, excerpt, date, category, author, readTime, tags, featuredImage\n\n### Gotchas\n- Content HTML must stay under 8000 characters for performance\n- All blog post fields are required; missing fields cause TypeScript compilation errors\n- Category must match existing categories or create new ones (checked against unique set)\n\n### Test results\n- Content size: 6,066 characters (well under 8,000 limit)\n- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (67 static pages including new blog post)\n- Blog post accessible via /blog/singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians slug\n- All acceptance criteria met:\n  - ✓ Slug and category correct\n  - ✓ Comparison table with all 3 pass types and salary minimums\n  - ✓ FAQ section with 5 Q&As (exceeds 3 required)\n  - ✓ ReadTime '12 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n  - ✓ npm run build passes\n\n## Iteration US-196 - Fri, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Integrate channel manager for live OTA availability synchronisation (ID: US-196)\n\n### What was implemented\n- **Beds24 client** (`site/src/lib/beds24-client.ts`):\n  - Implemented `parseAvailabilityResponse()` to parse real Beds24 API responses\n  - Returns proper `ChannelManagerAvailability` objects with room inventory\n  - Graceful fallback to placeholder data when channel manager is not configured\n- **ARI endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/ari/route.ts`):\n  - Fetches live availability from Beds24 API\n  - Maps to standard ARI format for connectivity partners (Google Free Booking Links)\n  - Returns JSON with property info, rooms, rates, and booking URL\n- **Booking sync endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/bookings/sync/route.ts`):\n  - Accepts POST requests with booking details (checkin, checkout, guest info, room type)\n  - Syncs bookings to Beds24 to close availability across OTAs\n  - Includes availability check via GET with query parameters\n- **Webhook endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/webhooks/beds24/route.ts`):\n  - Processes Beds24 webhook events for availability updates, booking creation/cancellation\n  - Validates webhook signature using HMAC-SHA256\n  - Logs event details (extensible for database updates)\n- **Configuration & types**:\n  - Channel manager config with environment variables\n  - Type definitions for availability, bookings, webhooks\n  - Updated .env.local.example with all required secrets\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Next.js App Router uses async GET/POST functions\n- Beds24 API response structure uses `roomtypes` key with room availability objects\n- Response parsing should handle multiple field names (id/roomId, name/roomName) for flexibility\n- Placeholder data allows testing without configured channel manager\n\n### Gotchas\n- Beds24 API field names vary (id vs roomId, total vs totalInventory)\n- Parser must be defensive and return empty array on malformed responses\n- Webhook validation using HMAC requires exact payload matching (must use `request.text()` not `request.json()`)\n\n### Test results\n- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (all 65 static pages generated)\n- ARI endpoint: ✓ shows as dynamic route (ƒ)\n- Webhook endpoint: ✓ shows as dynamic route (ƒ)\n- Booking sync endpoint: ✓ shows as dynamic route (ƒ)\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-196 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story is for a hotel channel manager integration, which seems mismatched with the `lhdn_payroll_integration` app context. The following brief assumes the target is a hospitality-focused Frappe app (e.g., `pelangi_hospitality`).\n\n**Project:** US-196: Channel Manager Integration\n**Target App:** `pelangi_hospitality` (Assumed)\n\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/channel_manager.py` to encapsulate all communication with the third-party channel manager API (e.g., Beds24).\n*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/webhooks.py` to create a dedicated, whitelisted endpoint to receive incoming ARI updates and bookings from the channel manager.\n*   **Existing File:** `pelangi_hospitality/doctype/booking/booking.py` (or similar for reservations) to add logic that pushes new bookings to the channel manager.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   **DocTypes:**\n    *   A new singleton `Channel Manager Settings` DocType is needed to store API keys, endpoint URLs, and credentials.\n    *   The `Booking` DocType will require modification to trigger syncs and store the channel manager's external booking ID.\n    *   The `Room` DocType needs a field to map its ID to the corresponding ID in the channel manager.\n*   **Hooks (`hooks.py`):**\n    *   `on_submit` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and reduce availability.\n    *   `on_cancel` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and increase availability.\n\n**3. Implementation Approach:**\n1.  Implement the API client with methods for fetching and updating availability.\n2.  Use the `Booking` DocType's server-side `after_insert` and `on_trash` events to trigger API calls.\n3.  Create a whitelisted API endpoint for the website frontend to call, which in turn fetches live availability from the channel manager API.\n4.  Implement the webhook endpoint to create/update local `Booking` documents from incoming OTA bookings.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Race Conditions:** Implement a locking mechanism or re-verify availability with the channel manager API just before creating a booking to prevent overbookings.\n*   **API Failure:** Use Frappe's background jobs (RQ) with a retry mechanism for all outgoing API calls to handle transient network issues or API downtime.\n*   **Webhook Security:** Validate incoming webhooks using a shared secret to prevent unauthorized requests.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-196 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story is for a hotel channel manager integration, which seems mismatched with the `lhdn_payroll_integration` app context. The following brief assumes the target is a hospitality-focused Frappe app (e.g., `pelangi_hospitality`).\n\n**Project:** US-196: Channel Manager Integration\n**Target App:** `pelangi_hospitality` (Assumed)\n\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/channel_manager.py` to encapsulate all communication with the third-party channel manager API (e.g., Beds24).\n*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/webhooks.py` to create a dedicated, whitelisted endpoint to receive incoming ARI updates and bookings from the channel manager.\n*   **Existing File:** `pelangi_hospitality/doctype/booking/booking.py` (or similar for reservations) to add logic that pushes new bookings to the channel manager.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   **DocTypes:**\n    *   A new singleton `Channel Manager Settings` DocType is needed to store API keys, endpoint URLs, and credentials.\n    *   The `Booking` DocType will require modification to trigger syncs and store the channel manager's external booking ID.\n    *   The `Room` DocType needs a field to map its ID to the corresponding ID in the channel manager.\n*   **Hooks (`hooks.py`):**\n    *   `on_submit` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and reduce availability.\n    *   `on_cancel` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and increase availability.\n\n**3. Implementation Approach:**","numLines":300,"startLine":0,"totalLines":1252}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01CSGB4UAzLpxhWaezKURRFM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Perfect. Now I understand:\n\n1. **Story US-173** is about implementing A/B testing with Vercel Feature Flags and Edge Config\n2. The story was previously skipped with an oversized diff (12854 lines, limit 500)\n3. The project is pelangi-website (Next.js + React)\n\nFrom the progress.txt patterns, I can see:\n- Projects use TypeScript, Next.js App Router, React Server Components\n- Environment variables are used for configuration\n- The codebase follows proper TS/React patterns\n- Stories need to be kept under ~500 lines of diff\n\n**Story Requirements (US-173):**\n- Create Vercel Edge Config store and connect to project\n- Install @vercel/edge-config and @vercel/flags packages\n- Define A/B flag for /book page primary CTA copy (e.g., 'Book Now' vs 'Check Availability' vs 'Get Best Price Direct')\n- Flag evaluated in Next.js Middleware, variant cookie set before page render (zero layout shift)\n- GA4 custom dimension records variant for statistical analysis\n- Vercel Flags UI dashboard shows flag status, rollout percentage, per-user override capability\n\n**Key technical notes:**\n- Install: npm install @vercel/edge-config @vercel/flags\n- Read flag in middleware.ts: import { get } from '@vercel/edge-config'; const variant = await get('cta-variant')\n- Pass variant via x-variant response header or cookie to Server Component for conditional rendering\n- Flags as Code pattern: define flag types in flags.ts, resolve server-side only\n\nThe problem with the previous attempt was likely that it was too comprehensive. The story was marked as \"Consistently oversized diff (12854 lines, limit 500)\". This suggests the prior attempt tried to do too much or touched unrelated files.\n\nI need to create a focused, minimal implementation that:\n\n\n1. Sets up the flags infrastructure\n2. Defines the A/B test flag for CTA copy\n3. Evaluates it in middleware\n4. Sets a cookie\n5. Uses it in the /book page\n6. Records the variant in GA4\n\nThe key is to be surgical—only touching the essential files: middleware.ts, flags.ts, the /book page component, and GA4 tracking code. I'll avoid refactoring or modifying unrelated parts of the codebase.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5792,"cache_read_input_tokens":56338,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5792},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5db64454-1df5-4c1c-9e81-ebba85b5ca40","uuid":"007de6f2-95c5-44db-b7da-811c01abc9a5"}
